{"name":"Green Weed","hex":"#548f6f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"weed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant unwanted at the place where and at the time when it is growing.","Ellipsis of duckweed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove unwanted vegetation from a cultivated area (especially grass).","To pilfer the best items from a collection."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/weed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Weed","blocks":[{"text":"A weed is a plant considered undesirable in a particular situation, growing where it conflicts with human preferences, needs, or goals. Plants with characteristics that make them hazardous, aesthetically unappealing, difficult to control in managed environments, or otherwise unwanted in farm land, orchards, gardens, lawns, parks, recreational spaces, residential and industrial areas, may all be considered weeds. The concept of weeds is particularly significant in agriculture, where the presence of weeds in fields used to grow crops may cause major losses in yields. Invasive species, plants introduced to an environment where their presence negatively impacts the overall functioning and biodiversity of the ecosystem, may also sometimes be considered weeds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Stairs_with_weed.jpg/330px-Stairs_with_weed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weed","wikidata":"Q101879"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg/500px-Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg/1280px-Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg/500px-Pusteblume_%2866278071%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A dandelion is a common plant all over the world, especially in Europe, Asia, and the Americas. It is considered a weed in some contexts (such as lawns) but not others (such as when it is grown as a vegetable or herbal medicine).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APusteblume_(66278071).jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg/500px-Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg/1280px-Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg/500px-Kanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Invasive Canada Goldenrod as a roadside weed in Poland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKanadische_Goldrute%2C_Solidago_canadensis_-_Invasives_Unkraut.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Cattle_poison.jpg/500px-Cattle_poison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Cattle_poison.jpg/1280px-Cattle_poison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Cattle_poison.jpg/500px-Cattle_poison.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australia, 1907: Cattlemen survey 700 carcasses of cattle that were killed overnight by a poisonous plant.[39]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACattle_poison.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Trifolium_repens_macro.jpg/500px-Trifolium_repens_macro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, http